        "es": {
          "intro": "Los prefijos modifican el significado, pero no cualquier combinación es válida. Underrated merece más reconocimiento; overrated recibe demasiado. Congest forma congestion y renew forma renewal.",
          "goals": [
            "Explica el significado.",
            "Reconoce la estructura.",
            "Crea tu propio ejemplo."
          ]
        },
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Prefixes: recognize accepted words",
            "body": "| Group | General meaning | Examples and definitions |\n|---|---|---|\n| un-, non-, ir-, im-, de- | negation, opposition or reversal | unattractive: not attractive; non-conformity: refusal to conform; irreplaceable: impossible to replace; impossible: not possible; degenerate: deteriorate |\n| under-, over- | insufficient / excessive degree | underrated: not valued highly enough; overexposed: given too much exposure |\n| mal-, mis- | bad or incorrect | maladministration: poor administration; mismanage: manage badly |\n| pre-, post- | before / after | pre-date: exist before; post-war: after a war |\n| pro-, anti- | in favor of / against | proactive: taking initiative; anti-establishment: opposed to the established system |\n| bi-, co-, inter- | two / jointly / between | bilingual: using two languages; co-founder: joint founder; international: between nations |\n| mini-, out-, semi- | small / surpass / half or partly | minibar: small drinks supply; outperform: perform better; semi-retired: partly retired |\n| sub-, super- | below or subordinate / above or exceptional | sub-zero: below zero; superpower: exceptional power or a very powerful nation |\n\nThe complete word determines the meaning: *proactive* means taking initiative, rather than simply “in favor of being active.” Roots usually retain their spelling, but hyphens and other adjustments may occur. There is no universal rule allowing any prefix to be attached to any root. Learn accepted words: *impossible*, not *unpossible*. More than one prefix can occur: *uncooperative* contains **un-** and **co-**.\n\n**Meaning contrasts:** *underrated* deserves more recognition; *overrated* receives too much. *Uninhabitable* means unsuitable to live in; *inhabited* means occupied; *inhabitable* means suitable to live in. *Bimonthly* can mean either every two months or twice a month outside the exercise; use *twice a month* when precision matters.",
            "es": {
              "title": "Prefixes: recognize accepted words",
              "body": "Los prefijos modifican el significado, pero no cualquier combinación es válida. Underrated merece más reconocimiento; overrated recibe demasiado. Congest forma congestion y renew forma renewal."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "City life and word formation",
            "body": "*Amenities* are facilities or services that make an area pleasant to use, such as parks, libraries or sports facilities. *Infrastructure* means the underlying networks and systems, such as transport, water and communications. *Traffic congestion* means excessive traffic that slows movement; *tolls* are charges for using roads; *urban renewal* is improvement of a built-up area; *abandonment* means leaving something unused or uncared for. *Built environment* refers to human-made surroundings; *visual clutter* means distracting visual information; *loitering* means remaining somewhere without an obvious purpose.\n\n*The proposal would improve local amenities and reduce traffic congestion.* This combines a proposed action with two community benefits.\n\nFrom the exercise: *abandon → abandonment; congest → congestion; renew → renewal*. A prefix comes before a base, a suffix after it. S3.4 contains *environment/environmental, regenerate/regeneration, drama/dramatic, clarify/clarification* and *object/objective*. Stress may move when a word is derived: *reGENerate → regenerAtion*. The script supplies the word pairs, but audio is needed to check their exact spoken realization.\n\nThe p. 38 article reports studies and historical examples. Practise summarizing **what the text reports**, without automatically treating each example as a present-day recommendation for your city.",
            "es": {
              "title": "City life and word formation",
              "body": "Los prefijos modifican el significado, pero no cualquier combinación es válida. Underrated merece más reconocimiento; overrated recibe demasiado. Congest forma congestion y renew forma renewal."
            }
          }
        ]
